### Why can't you just deploy the old binary?

Code reverts instantly. Data does not. Every row the new version wrote stays in the new format.

```java
// New version writes amount as integer paise in a new column
row.setAmountMinor(amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact());
row.setAmount(null); // stops writing old column

// Old binary expects the old decimal column:
BigDecimal amount = row.getBigDecimal("amount"); // NULL → wrong result!
```

### What is expand-and-contract?

Three phases that ensure rollback is always safe.

#### Phase 1: Expand (additive + dual-write)

```sql
-- Additive schema change: ADD, do not DROP
ALTER TABLE transfers ADD COLUMN amount_minor BIGINT;
-- Old column stays; new column added
```

```java
// Dual-write: BOTH formats maintained
void applyAmount(TransferRow row, BigDecimal amount) {
    row.setAmount(amount);                                   // OLD format
    row.setAmountMinor(amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()); // NEW format
}
// Now either binary can run: old reads amount, new reads amount_minor
```

#### Phase 2: Coexist

- Run new code but keep producing old format.
- Verify both formats agree continuously.
- Backfill historical rows in throttled batches.
- If a bug is found, rollback is SAFE because the old format was never abandoned.

```java
// Backfill old rows into new format
for (TransferRow row : repo.findWhereAmountMinorIsNull()) {
    row.setAmountMinor(row.getAmount().movePointRight(2).longValueExact());
    repo.save(row);
}
```

#### Phase 3: Contract (separate, much later deploy)

```java
// ONLY after you will NOT roll back
void applyAmount(TransferRow row, BigDecimal amount) {
    row.setAmountMinor(amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()); // NEW only
    // row.setAmount(...) removed
}
// ALTER TABLE transfers DROP COLUMN amount;
```

This is the one irreversible step. Done as a separate deploy, long after the rollout.

### How does this look as a timeline?

```text
  Deploy 1 (Expand): ADD new column, dual-write
       │
       ▼ (days/weeks of coexistence)
  Verify: both formats agree ✓
  Backfill: all old rows have new format ✓
  Confidence: high ✓
       │
       ▼
  Deploy 2 (Contract): DROP old column, stop dual-write
       ← rollback no longer safe after this point
```

### Does this apply beyond DB schemas?

| Contract Type | Expand | Contract |
|---|---|---|
| DB column | Add new column, dual-write | Drop old column |
| API field | Add new field, keep old | Remove old field |
| Event schema | Produce both versions | Stop producing old version |
| Cache layout | Write both formats | Remove old format |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: combining expand and contract in one deploy
// New version drops old column AND writes new → rollback impossible

// CORRECT: expand and contract are SEPARATE deploys
// Expand: add new, keep old (reversible)
// ... time passes, confidence builds ...
// Contract: remove old (irreversible, deliberate)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The cost is temporary duplication (two formats, dual writes, verification).
- That cost is the price of being able to undo a bad deploy without data loss.
- For a bank, this is non-negotiable.
- The quiet failure (old code reads new data wrong and produces wrong financial results) is far more dangerous than a loud crash.
